# Machine-local paths for hook commands in settings.json.
#
# Written by import.sh step 12, or on demand by write-paths-local.sh in this
# directory. This example is the shape; the filled-in copy stays out of git —
# ~/.claude is not a repo and export.sh reads only settings.json.
#
# Hook commands source this file and exit 2 — blocking the tool call — when a
# path it should define is missing.

# This config folder's agent profile: personal or work. dev-env's import.sh builds
# settings.json from the base plus agents/claude/overlays/<profile>*.json, export.sh
# checks the live file against the same, and agents-shared's init-global.sh links the
# profile's rules and skills. One machine may hold several folders with different profiles.
AGENT_PROFILE="personal"

# Checkout of the agents-shared repo, which holds hooks/ and claude/rules/.
# Whatever this machine actually uses, e.g. $HOME/dev/agents-shared or
# $HOME/src/agents-shared or $HOME/work/tools/agents-shared.
AGENTS_SHARED_DIR="$HOME/path/to/agents-shared"

# Checkout of the system-one repo, which holds the decision-model wrapper, its hooks and skill.
# Personal profile only: its hook is in overlays/personal.json.
SYSTEM_ONE_DIR="$HOME/path/to/system-one"

# Checkout of agent-request-limiter, set by its install.sh on machines that run it. The
# SessionStart check sources it; unset means the limiter is not installed here and the check
# does nothing. Personal profile only, like SYSTEM_ONE_DIR.
# ARL_DIR="$HOME/path/to/agent-request-limiter"

# cc-status binary (iTerm2 tab-status integration), set on machines that have it installed.
# Every cc-status hook sources this file and exits 0 quietly if unset — it's optional.
# CC_STATUS_PATH="$HOME/.config/iterm2/cc-status"
